# motion_exists: S2 speaks of motion "evident to our senses", while S4 widens motion to any reduction from potentiality to actuality. Sensed motion is a case of that wider kind, so the claim's truth conditions do not split between the statements.
# moved_by_another: S9 only excludes self-motion "in the same respect", so "another" might mean another part or respect rather than a distinct thing. But the text uses "another" uniformly, and S11 treats it as a distinct mover in a chain. Unsure, but I judge it one sense.
# first_mover: in S12 "first" is the principal mover on which subsequent movers depend (hand and staff). Every use, including S13, keeps "put in motion by no other", so I read it as one sense.
